narrative:
## Modeling a Recipe

A recipe has a title, servings, and ingredients. Each ingredient has a name, amount, and unit. Structs let you capture this hierarchy.

### The Ingredient Struct

```xiom
type Ingredient = {
  name: Str;
  amount: Float64;
  unit: Str;
}
```

### The Recipe Struct

```xiom
type Recipe = {
  title: Str;
  servings: Int;
  main_ingredient: Ingredient;
}
```

For now, we store one ingredient directly in the recipe (in later lessons you'll learn collections for multiple ingredients).

### Creating Recipes

```xiom
let flour = Ingredient{ name: "Flour"; amount: 2.5; unit: "cups"; };

let pancakes = Recipe{
  title: "Pancakes";
  servings: 4;
  main_ingredient: flour;
};
```

### Structs as Pure Data

Not every struct needs methods. Some exist just to group related data together — a **data-only struct**. You create them, pass them around, and access their fields.

```xiom
let recipe_name = pancakes.title;           // "Pancakes"
let ingredient_name = flour.name;           // "Flour"
let total_flour = flour.amount * 2.0;       // 5.0 (double batch)
```

### When to Add Methods

If you later need to scale a recipe (multiply all amounts by a factor), you'd add a method. But start simple — data-only structs are valid and common.

